 "unit": "EUR bn, current prices, ECB annual USD/EUR as trade_full.json; 2026_JanAug not annualised",
 "replaces": "reexports.json hubs.{TUR,AZE,SRB}: reexports_to_RUS, reexports_to_RUS_baseline_2019_2021, reexport_share_of_exports_to_RUS (same shape). Origin shares, margins, onward-Russian-goods fields stay. IMPORTANT: apply_reexports() multiplies these series by refine.json reexport_share[hub][y][b]/old; if the series are swapped, delete those three hubs from refine.json reexport_share (or set the factor to 1). Alternatively keep reexports.json and swap refine.json reexport_share.{TUR,AZE,SRB} for refine_reexport_share_equivalent (same effect, no code change). apply_residual() reads reexport_share_of_exports_to_RUS directly (without the refine factor) for the pro-rata gap moved to origins.",
 "method": "Per HS6 product p (chapters as reexports.json), from the hub's own Comtrade report: XR = exports to Russia; M3 = imports from all partners minus Russia; XW = exports to all partners minus Russia. Base 2021 (2019 not pulled: quota). Matched increase m_p = max(0, min(dXR, dM3)) vs 2021. Weight w_p = 1 - XW/M3 in 2021 (clamped 0-1; a product the hub already exported to the rest of the world counts as home production). Re-exports_y = sum w_p m_p + pre-war level sum w_p min(XR_2021, M3_2021). Low uses w_p^2 (both parts); high = max(level capacity sum min(XR_y, max(0, M3_y - XW_y)), central x1.3) = most foreign content the hub's own imports allow. 2026 Jan-Aug = 2025 share of exports to Russia x trade_full Jan-Aug 2026, range x0.8/x1.2. HS2021->HS2022 code changes between 2021 (H5) and 2022+ (H6) are treated as new/lost codes (min of both sides, small).",
